WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.740
My name is Justin.
I work for Perspective Tester,

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.560
and I'm blind or low vision,

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.680
and I'm a native
assistive technology user.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.360
My vision presents with no central vision
and limited peripheral vision.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.900
Without assistive technology,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.640
I certainly don't think I would be
where I am today.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.560
You know, I can't see the screen
when it's back this far.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.399
I see a little bit of light
and contrast in certain places,

00:00:22.439 --> 00:00:25.800
but I really need to get down
on the individual letter size

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.319
to see what things are.

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:30.719
I use screen readers and zoom
and all the different tools

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:35.000
to help me navigate life and to be
as independent as I possibly can.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.900
And so when it doesn't work,
it's extremely frustrating.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.900
It's pretty limiting, but I can
still function and get around.

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:44.080
With nothing in the middle,
I can't see my screens very well.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.540
I use assistive technology
in a myriad of different ways.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.199
When I'm using my computer,

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:54.760
I'm zooming down to individual letters
and navigating my screens that way.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.180
Sometimes it's really difficult
to read long paragraphs of text

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.099
with one letter on the screen at a time,

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.479
and so I use VoiceOver
at a pretty fast speaking rate.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:08.079
It's really important that a lot
of websites and mobile apps

00:01:08.119 --> 00:01:12.580
work well with screen readers,
to have it read it to me.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.719
It's really amazing,
but when it doesn't work,

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:17.859
it creates huge limiting factors.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.420
My name's Justin,
and this is my perspective.
